Day 253/250: Divide an Array Into Subarrays With Minimum Cost II

Problem Statement #3013

Problem Statement Explantion:

You are given an array of integers nums of length n, and two positive integers k and dist.

The cost of an array is the value of its first element. For example, the cost of [1,2,3] is 1.

You need to divide nums into k disjoint contiguous subarrays such that the difference between the starting index of the second subarray and the starting index of the last subarray is at most dist. 
In other words, if the starting indices of the k subarrays are i1,i2,…,ik, then ik−i2≤dist.
Return the minimum possible sum of the costs of these subarrays.

Solution Approach:

1. The Core Insight
The first subarray always starts at index 0. Therefore, nums[0] is always part of our cost. We need to pick k−1 additional starting indices from the remaining array.
The constraint ik-i2≤dist means that all chosen starting indices (from the 2nd to the k-th) must fit within a window of size dist + 1.

2. Strategy
We use a Sliding Window of size dist + 1 starting from index 1. For every window, we want to find the m=k−1 smallest elements.
To maintain the m smallest elements efficiently as the window slides, we use two Balanced BSTs (implemented via TreeSet in Java):
Left Set: Stores the m smallest elements currently in the window.
Right Set: Stores the remaining elements in the window that are not among the m smallest.

3. Balancing Logic
Add/Remove: When the window moves, remove the element that is out of range and add the new element.
Maintain Size: If the Left Set has fewer than m elements, pull the smallest from the Right Set.
Swap for Optimality: If the smallest element in the Right Set is smaller than the largest in the Left Set, swap them to ensure the Left Set always contains the absolute minimums.

Here's the code:

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public long minimumCost(int[] nums, int k, int dist) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int m = k - 1; 
        long currentSmallestSum = 0;
        
        Comparator<Integer> cmp = (a, b) -> nums[a] != nums[b] ? 
                                    Integer.compare(nums[a], nums[b]) : Integer.compare(a, b);
        
        TreeSet<Integer> left = new TreeSet<>(cmp);
        TreeSet<Integer> right = new TreeSet<>(cmp);

        for (int i = 1; i <= dist + 1; i++) {
            left.add(i);
            currentSmallestSum += nums[i];
        }

        while (left.size() > m) {
            int last = left.pollLast();
            currentSmallestSum -= nums[last];
            right.add(last);
        }

        long minCost = currentSmallestSum;

        for (int i = dist + 2; i < n; i++) {
            int outIdx = i - dist - 1;
            if (left.contains(outIdx)) {
                currentSmallestSum -= nums[outIdx];
                left.remove(outIdx);
            } else {
                right.remove(outIdx);
            }

            left.add(i);
            currentSmallestSum += nums[i];

            while (left.size() > m) {
                int last = left.pollLast();
                currentSmallestSum -= nums[last];
                right.add(last);
            }
            
            while (!right.isEmpty() && nums[right.first()] < nums[left.last()]) {
                int fromLeft = left.pollLast();
                int fromRight = right.pollFirst();
                
                currentSmallestSum -= nums[fromLeft];
                currentSmallestSum += nums[fromRight];
                
                right.add(fromLeft);
                left.add(fromRight);
            }

            minCost = Math.min(minCost, currentSmallestSum);
        }

        return nums[0] + minCost;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(nlog(dist))
Space Complexity: O(dist)



